\pard\f0\fs22\lang1033 A version control system (VCS) is a tool that tracks changes to files and directories over time. It allows multiple contributors to collaborate on a project by managing changes, maintaining a history of revisions, and enabling the synchronization of work across different locations or among different team members. There are two main types of version control systems: centralized and distributed.\par
\par
we have two type of version control system\par
--------------------------------------------\par
1)Centralized Version Control Systems (CVCS):\par
2)Distributed Version Control Systems(DVCS)\par
\par
----GIT----\par
Overall, Git is a powerful and widely used version control system that plays a crucial role in modern software development workflows, enabling efficient collaboration, code management, and version control across teams of developers.\par
\par
\b Note\b0 : git is a version control system tools that  helps to track changes in code s\par
\par
---- GitHub:----\par
\par
GitHub is a web-based platform built around Git, providing hosting for Git repositories along with collaboration features and tools for software development projects.\par
It serves as a central hub for hosting Git repositories in the cloud, allowing users to store, manage, and share their code online.\par
GitHub offers collaboration features such as pull requests, issues, project boards, wikis, and code review tools, enabling teams to work together effectively on projects.\par
It also provides social features like user profiles, activity feeds, and notifications, fostering a vibrant community of developers.git\par
GitHub offers additional features such as integrations with third-party services, security features like branch protection and vulnerability scanning, and deployment options through services like GitHub Actions.\par
\par
\par
\cf1\ul\b Git & GitHub Terminologies (Simple List)\ulnone\par
\cf0\b0\par
\cf2 Repository (Repo)\par
\cf0 A storage location for your project code.\par
\par
\cf2 Git\par
\cf0 A version control tool used to track code changes.\par
\par
GitHub\par
An online platform used to store Git repositories.\par
\par
\cf2 Working Directory\par
\cf0 The project folder where you write and modify code.\par
\par
\cf2 Staging Area\par
\cf0 A temporary area where changes are kept before committing.\par
\par
\cf2 Commit\par
\cf0 A saved snapshot of your code changes.\par
\par
\cf2 Commit ID\par
\cf0 A unique number generated for each commit.\par
\par
\cf2 Branch\par
\cf0 A separate line of development used for new features or fixes.\par

\pard{\pntext\f4\'B7\tab}{\*\pn\pnlvlblt\pnf4\pnindent0{\pntxtb\'B7}}\fi-360\li720 git branch / git branch -a --> to check branch\par
{\pntext\f4\'B7\tab}git branch branch-name --> create new branch\par
{\pntext\f4\'B7\tab}git branch -M main --> rename branch\par
{\pntext\f4\'B7\tab}git checkout branch-name / git switch branch-name --> Switch to a Branch\par
{\pntext\f4\'B7\tab}git checkout -b branch-name / git switch -c branch-name --> Create and Switch to a Branch\par

\pard\par
\par
\par
\cf2 Main/Master Branch\par
\cf0 The primary branch containing stable code.\par
\par
\cf2 HEAD\par
\cf0 Points to the current branch and latest commit.\par
\par
\cf2 Clone\par
\cf0 Copying a repository from GitHub to your local machine.\par
\par
\cf2 Push\par
\cf0 Uploading local code changes to GitHub.\par
\par
\cf2 Pull\par
\cf0 git pull is used to download the latest changes from a remote repository (GitHub/Bitbucket) and update your local branch.\par
\par
\cf2 Fetch\par
\cf0 Downloading changes from GitHub without merging.\par
\par
\cf2 Merge\par
\cf0 Combining changes from one branch into another.\par
\par
\cf2 Merge Conflict\par
\cf0 Happens when Git cannot automatically merge changes.\par
\par
\cf2 Rebase\par
\cf0 Moves commits to create a cleaner project history.\par
\par
\cf2 Checkout\par
\cf0 Switching from one branch to another.\par
\par
\cf2 Tag\par
\cf0 Used to mark a specific version or release.\par
\par
\cf2 Stash\par
\cf0 Temporarily stores uncommitted changes.\par
\par
\cf2 Reset\par
\cf0 Moves code back to a previous commit.\par
gir reset / git reset filename\par
 \par
\par
\cf2 Revert\par
\cf0 Undoes a commit by creating a new commit.\par
\par
\cf2 .gitignore\par
\cf0 File used to ignore unwanted files and folders.\par
\par
